Title = {The heavy burden of city life: Factors affecting mercury concentration
   in urban red squirrels},
Journal = {ECOLOGICAL INDICATORS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {172},
Month = {MAR},
Abstract = {Mercury is one of the most harmful metal contaminants, potentially
   affecting population functioning. We assessed the use of the red
   squirrel as a bioindicator of Hg contamination in the urban habitats of
   Warsaw. We thus compared total mercury (THg) concentrations in hair
   samples of live-trapped squirrels inhabiting areas of different mercury
   pollution: the urban forest in Warsaw's outer district and the urban
   parkin the city center, including in the analysis factors that may
   influence mercury concentration, i.e., age, body mass, reproductive
   status, and season. Mercury determination was done in a laboratory using
   atomic absorption spectrometry. In general, the concentration of THg in
   hair samples from sub-adult individuals was lower than in adults. The
   site of a sample collection and the body mass of squirrels in the urban
   park positively affected THg contamination. The higher mercury levels in
   the urban park were in line with the pollution levels in the soil. They
   can be explained by current and/or historical pollution and possibly
   also by park squirrels' altered behavior. On the contrary, the season
   when the samples were taken, sex, or reproductive status did not
   influence the THg level, suggesting that the red squirrel can be a
   useful bioindicator species of Hg contamination in urban areas.
   Although, in general, the mercury levels were moderate, elevated levels
   in the park suggest that mercury remains a problem and should be
   monitored in urban areas.},
