   Dalia and Tumas, Natalia and Miranda, J. Jaime},
Title = {The urban built environment and adult BMI, obesity, and diabetes in
   Latin American cities},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2022},
Volume = {13},
Number = {1},
Month = {DEC 29},
Abstract = {Latin America is the world's most urbanized region and its heterogeneous
   urban development may impact chronic diseases. Here, we evaluated the
   association of built environment characteristics at the sub-city
   -intersection density, greenness, and population density- and city-level
   -fragmentation and isolation- with body mass index (BMI), obesity, and
   type 2 diabetes (T2D). Data from 93,280 (BMI and obesity) and 122,211
   individuals (T2D) was analysed across 10 countries. Living in areas with
   higher intersection density was positively associated with BMI and
   obesity, whereas living in more fragmented and greener areas were
   negatively associated. T2D was positively associated with intersection
   density, but negatively associated with greenness and population
   density. The rapid urban expansion experienced by Latin America provides
   unique insights and vastly expand opportunities for population-wide
   urban interventions aimed at reducing obesity and T2D burden.},
